 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvnotrims"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#40921"
-msgid "Dolby Vision metadata carries the trims of the film: how the colourist mapped each scene to displays of a few given peak brightnesses (levels 2 and 8 of the metadata). The display is sent them unless this is on. Turn it on only for a display that shows Dolby Vision with washed-out blacks or wrong colours because of them, as some projectors do: it then maps each scene from its brightness range alone, as it does with metadata that has no trims. The picture that is sent does not change. Applies from the next video."
+msgid "Dolby Vision data include the colourist's fine-tuning (trims): how each scene was adjusted for displays of a few given peak brightnesses. They are sent to your display unless this is on. Turn it on only if your display shows Dolby Vision with washed-out blacks or wrong colours because of them, as some projectors do: it then maps each scene from its brightness range alone. The picture that is sent does not change. Off by default. Takes effect from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10plus" (group "Dolby Vision")
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#40922"
-msgid "HDR10+ as Dolby Vision"
+msgid "Play HDR10+ videos as Dolby Vision"
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10plus"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#40923"
-msgid "For a display that takes Dolby Vision but not HDR10+. An HDR10+ video is sent as Dolby Vision, with metadata made from its own HDR10+ metadata, scene by scene: the peak and the average brightness of each scene. The picture is the same HDR10 picture. What does not carry over from HDR10+: the tone curve and the target display that the studio chose, its saturation weight and its windows; the darkest level is taken as black, and the average is worked out from the brightness distribution that HDR10+ gives. Dolby Vision videos, HDR10 videos without HDR10+ and displays that take HDR10+ are not affected. Applies from the next video."
+msgid "For a TV that supports Dolby Vision but not HDR10+. An HDR10+ video is sent as Dolby Vision, with metadata made from its own HDR10+ data, scene by scene (the peak and the average brightness of each scene). The picture is the same HDR10 picture. What does not carry over from HDR10+: the tone curve and the target display that the studio chose, its saturation weight and its windows. Dolby Vision videos, HDR10 videos without HDR10+ and displays that take HDR10+ are not affected. Off by default. Takes effect from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10" (group "Dolby Vision")
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#40924"
-msgid "HDR10 as Dolby Vision, measured"
+msgid "Make HDR10 videos Dolby Vision (measured)"
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvfromhdr10"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#40925"
-msgid "For a display that takes Dolby Vision. An HDR10 video, which has no dynamic metadata of its own, is sent as Dolby Vision with metadata that Hades measures from the picture as it plays: the peak, the average and the darkest level of each scene, and the black bars. These metadata are not the studio's: the studio made no Dolby Vision metadata for the video, and nothing of a colourist's grade (trims) is added. The picture is the same HDR10 picture. Scene cuts are found from the picture: a few are missed, a few are found where there is none, and some are found a picture late. Dolby Vision and HDR10+ videos are not affected. Applies from the next video."
+msgid "For a TV that supports Dolby Vision. An HDR10 video has no Dolby Vision data of its own, so Hades measures the picture as it plays (the peak, the average and the darkest level of each scene, and the black bars) and sends that as Dolby Vision metadata. These are not the studio's data: nothing of a colourist's grade (trims) is added. Scene cuts are found from the picture: a few are missed, a few are found where there is none, and some are found a picture late. The picture itself is the same HDR10 picture. Dolby Vision and HDR10+ videos are not affected. Off by default. Takes effect from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvashdr10plus"
@@ -26935,7 +26935,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvashdr10plus"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#40927"
-msgid "For a display that takes HDR10+ and not Dolby Vision, as many Samsung TVs. A Dolby Vision video rebuilt as HDR10 is sent as HDR10+: with each scene, dynamic metadata that Hades measures from the rebuilt picture (the average and the distribution of its brightness), so that the display adapts each scene as it adapts an HDR10+ film. The scenes are those of the Dolby Vision metadata. These metadata are not the studio's, and no tone curve is sent: the display makes its own. Profile 8.1 is then rebuilt too; profile 7 as the setting above says. The first picture of a scene waits for its own measurement where it comes before the next refresh, else it goes out with the metadata of the scene before; the display may show a scene otherwise than as plain HDR10. Not while the picture is mapped to the display. For one video, the video settings of the player can send it as plain HDR10 or as HDR10+, whatever this setting says. Applies from the next video."
+msgid "For a display that takes HDR10+ but not Dolby Vision, as many Samsung TVs. A Dolby Vision video rebuilt as HDR10 is sent as HDR10+, with scene-by-scene metadata that Hades measures from the rebuilt picture, so that the display adapts each scene as it does with an HDR10+ film. These data are not the studio's and no tone curve is sent: the display makes its own, and may show a scene differently from plain HDR10. Profile 8.1 is then rebuilt too. Not used while the picture is mapped to the display. For one video, the video settings of the player can send it as plain HDR10 or as HDR10+. Off by default. Takes effect from the next video."
 msgstr ""
 
 #. Hades: label of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvmenus"
@@ -26947,7 +26947,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesdvmenus"
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#40991"
-msgid "Keeps the TV in Dolby Vision while the menus are shown: a Dolby Vision video then starts without the TV changing its picture mode, and goes back to the menus the same way. Any other video (SDR, HDR10, HDR10+, HLG) makes the TV change its mode once more at its start and once more at its end. The menus are sent with fixed metadata, at the white of \"GUI and subtitle brightness in HDR\" (203 cd/m² at 100 %), and the TV shows them in its Dolby Vision picture mode, which may look different from its SDR one. While the menus change, the graphics chip converts each of their frames. Shown only for a display that takes Dolby Vision, with the menus at 3840x2160. If the TV shows a black or broken picture, turn this off."
+msgid "Keeps the TV in Dolby Vision while the menus are on screen, so a Dolby Vision video starts, and goes back to the menus, without the TV switching its picture mode. Any other video (SDR, HDR10, HDR10+, HLG) makes the TV switch once more at its start and once more at its end. The menus are sent at a fixed brightness (the white of 'GUI and subtitle brightness in HDR') and the TV shows them in its Dolby Vision picture mode, which may look different from its SDR one. While the menus change, the graphics chip converts each frame. Shown only for a TV that takes Dolby Vision, with the menus at 3840x2160. If the TV shows a black or broken picture, turn this off. Off by default."
 msgstr ""
 
